# A parameter its body asked about cannot be used at another type.
#
# `announce` calls `str`, which asks what it was given. `str` accepts an
# Int, a String or a Bool, but a signature can only name one of them, so
# the parameter is pinned to what the first call passed and the
# definition means `(Int) -> String` from then on.
#
# The second call is refused in the ordinary words: inference produces
# types the checker enforces, so a mismatch against an inferred type
# reads exactly like a mismatch against a written one.
#
# Annotating `announce` does not help today either -- there is no type
# to write. Saying "any type `str` accepts" needs something Pyking does
# not have yet.
#
# This program is expected to FAIL type checking. Its `.ast.out` holds
# the error message, not a program's output.

def announce(value):
    'got ' ++ str(value)

print(announce(1))
print(announce(true))
